import IO
import Session
/-!
# SessionDirect

Direct-style session-typed channels over `IO1`: `import SessionDirect` (stage 25i,
docs/stages/25-concurrency.md).

`lib/Session.wiz` (25b–c) passes every endpoint to a continuation. Here each operation
*returns* the endpoint at its next step, at quantity 1, through `IO1` (an action whose
result is linear), and `do1` sequences the steps:

```
def ponger (1 c : Chan (Proto.dual PingPong)) : IO Unit := do1
  (n, c) ⇐ Chan.recvD c;
  c ⇐ Chan.sendD c (n + 1);
  Chan.closeD c
```

The endpoints are the same `Chan p` as `Session`'s, made by the same natives, so the two
styles mix freely on one channel: `Chan.send c a k` is `IO1.bindIO (Chan.sendD c a) k`
(`Chan.sendK` below proves the shape), and an endpoint made by `Chan.forkD` can be
handed to `Proc.run` or any CPS code. The converse (direct style from the CPS API alone)
can't be written: `IO`'s result is unrestricted, so a CPS continuation can't hand a
linear endpoint back out. That is why this file binds the natives itself, privately, the
way `Session` does.

Everything `Session` guarantees holds here for the same reasons: quantity 1 is exact, so
an endpoint is used exactly once (not copied, not dropped, not kept after its protocol
moved on); the types check every step; `forkD` is the only way to make a channel and it
gives the dual end to a new thread, so the channel graph stays a forest; deadlock freedom
is the meta-theorem of 25c, under the same conditions (no `MVar`/`Lock`, no `partial`).

Recursive protocols (25j) and delegation (25k) are below, on their own protocol type
`RProto`, since `Proto` can express neither.
-/

-- ## 1. Finite protocols, direct style (25i)

-- The natives, as in `Session`: they return the endpoint inside `IO`'s ω result, so they
-- are private, and the public operations below hand it on at quantity 1 (`IO.toIO1`).
private extern "wiz_chan_fork" Chan.forkRawD : {0 p : Proto} →
  (1 child : (1 c : Chan (Proto.dual p)) → IO Unit) → IO (Chan p)
private extern "wiz_chan_send" Chan.sendRawD : {0 A : Type} → {0 k : A → Proto} →
  (1 c : Chan (Proto.send A k)) → (a : A) → IO (Chan (k a))
private extern "wiz_chan_recv" Chan.recvRawD : {0 A : Type} → {0 k : A → Proto} →
  (1 c : Chan (Proto.recv A k)) → IO ((a : A) × Chan (k a))
private extern "wiz_chan_close" Chan.closeRawD : (1 c : Chan Proto.done) → IO Unit

/-- Make a channel following `p`: a new thread runs `child` on the dual end, and this
thread gets the other end back. `child` is linear, so it may capture other endpoints
(that is how an endpoint moves to another thread).

```
c ⇐ Chan.forkD PingPong ponger;
``` -/
def Chan.forkD (p : Proto) (1 child : (1 c : Chan (Proto.dual p)) → IO Unit) : IO1 (Chan p) :=
  IO.toIO1 (Chan.forkRawD child)

/-- Send `a`; the endpoint comes back at `k a`. Sending never blocks. -/
def Chan.sendD {A : Type} {k : A → Proto} (1 c : Chan (Proto.send A k)) (a : A) : IO1 (Chan (k a)) :=
  IO.toIO1 (Chan.sendRawD c a)

/-- Wait for a value; it comes back with the endpoint at `k a`. The value is unrestricted,
the endpoint linear: `(a, c) ⇐ Chan.recvD c;`. -/
def Chan.recvD {A : Type} {k : A → Proto} (1 c : Chan (Proto.recv A k)) : IO1 ((a : A) × Chan (k a)) :=
  IO.toIO1 (Chan.recvRawD c)

/-- Close a finished endpoint (it can't be dropped). -/
def Chan.closeD (1 c : Chan Proto.done) : IO Unit := Chan.closeRawD c

/-- Cancel at a send point of a cancellable protocol (`Proto.sendC`): tell the peer, and close. -/
def Chan.cancelD {A : Type} {k : A → Proto} (1 c : Chan (Proto.sendC A k)) : IO Unit := do1
  c ⇐ Chan.sendD c Msg.cancel;
  Chan.closeD c

-- ### The CPS operations, from the direct ones

/-- `Session`'s `Chan.send`, written over `Chan.sendD`: the CPS API is the direct one
followed by `IO1.bindIO`. -/
def Chan.sendK {A : Type} {k : A → Proto} {B : Type} (1 c : Chan (Proto.send A k)) (a : A)
    (1 next : (1 c' : Chan (k a)) → IO B) : IO B :=
  IO1.bindIO (Chan.sendD c a) next
/-- `Session`'s `Chan.recv`, over `Chan.recvD`. -/
def Chan.recvK {A : Type} {k : A → Proto} {B : Type} (1 c : Chan (Proto.recv A k))
    (1 next : (a : A) → (1 c' : Chan (k a)) → IO B) : IO B :=
  IO1.bindIO (Chan.recvD c) (fun r => let 1 (a, c') := r; next a c')
/-- `Session`'s `Chan.fork`, over `Chan.forkD`. -/
def Chan.forkK {B : Type} (p : Proto) (1 child : (1 c : Chan (Proto.dual p)) → IO Unit)
    (1 parent : (1 c : Chan p) → IO B) : IO B :=
  IO1.bindIO (Chan.forkD p child) parent

/-- A pure process (`Proc`, 25b) run over an endpoint in direct style: the same as
`Proc.run`, one `do1` step per protocol step. -/
def Proc.runD {R : Type} : (p : Proto) → Proc R p → (1 c : Chan p) → IO R
  | .send A k => fun x c => do1
      c ⇐ Chan.sendD c x.1;
      Proc.runD (k x.1) x.2 c
  | .recv A k => fun f c => do1
      (a, c) ⇐ Chan.recvD c;
      Proc.runD (k a) (f a) c
  | .done => fun r c => do1
      Chan.closeD c;
      IO.pure r
